 @Ravenousjoe  Right, for games released 11 years ago this is probably a 4k card. My comment towards pricing is not related to its profit margin compared to cards in the past. Heck, the Radeon VII was a HUGE die, with the VERY expensive HBM memory, it used alot of power, and thus, because it was on a brand new node, AMD easily could have charged $1500-$2000 for it, but because we werent in a crypto boom companies didnt have an incentive to vastly overprice their MSRP
If this were a normal market, the 6600xt would have a launch price between $250 and $329, with Nvidia cards launching with a similar price drop, and a few months after release we'd start to see drops in price to significantly below MSRP. But because we live in a crazy world with parts shortages due to reduced production, and sky high demand due to crypto, prices are not only at MSRP nearly a year after the launch of many of these cards, but we're seeing several models that are more than double MSRP
This is the sole reason the MSRP of these cards are this high, because they have a duty to their shareholders to squeeze the market for what it can bear. Its just sad that the market can bear to pay near top teir prices from 10-14 years ago, for a mid range card. In what world would that be acceptable. Would you be fine paying $116k for a mid level Toyota Camry because its faster than 1980 Porsche 928? No

 @denvera1g1  I am not comparing using older games, go look at benchmarks from the 480's release, it was a crap "1080p gpu" (since people insist on using useless marketing labels), and even years later with the 780, that gpu was barely able to be considered a 1080p card in relevant (at the time) games. This gpu is on a completely different level and to plenty would even be a great "1440p gpu", and cannot even be compared to older gpus 1:1.

I do agree they have raised prices a bit on this generation, but it is more in line with the idea that they have stagnated. The gpus you get now are basically the same as last Gen, just slightly more efficient with a "lower tier" name and the same price.

You really can't compare a car to a GPU though as there is a myriad of reasons to pick one car over another, and their retained or increased resale value can be traced back to things like rarity, availability at the time, and examples of said vehicle that are still in good shape. A GPU isn't special, there were thousands upon thousands made, and to the vast majority of people the only thing that makes them better or worse is the performance. A GTX 480 is a crap gpu now so it doesn't hold it's value because no one needs it for anything else except to churn out frames, a 1980s Porsche could be a beautiful center piece for a collection, a thrilling care to drive, or just an extremely rare product that had it's value increase over time because more people want it, but less and less are around. One is special, one is e-waste.

 @jonas6841  LMFAO. K. We will just ignore how AMD raised all their prices because of the original MSRP. We will also ignore that 6700xt costs $900-$1,000 while the 3080 costs $900-$1,200.

Don't get me wrong, I was excited to get a 6800xt until the price raised to $1,400.

Before you say anymore stupid shit, these are retail prices, not scalper
